   "evidence": "Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/BilinearForm/Orthogonal.lean:138-145 documents the chirality explicitly (\"for all `y` in `N`, `B y x = 0` \u2026 one could define a \"left\" orthogonal complement \u2026 not currently provided\"); `mem_orthogonal_iff` (line 150) gives `m \u2208 B.orthogonal N \u2194 \u2200 n \u2208 N, B n m = 0`. The file's own lines 249-255 derive `B w L.nPlus = 0` only from `hB : B.IsRefl`.",
   "file": "EpsilonEridani/Relativity/LightConeBasis.lean",
   "fix": "Say which argument carries the basis vector: e.g. `transverse` = `{v | B n\u208a v = 0 \u2227 B n\u208b v = 0}` (Mathlib's `orthogonal`), and note in the module docstring that `B w n\u208a = 0` holds only under `B.IsRefl`, pointing at `bilinForm_nPlus_eq_zero_of_mem_transverse` / `bilinForm_nMinus_eq_zero_of_mem_transverse` (which are themselves undocumented).",
   "issue": "`transverse` is documented as \"the orthogonal complement of `n\u208a` and `n\u208b`\" (also line 39 \"its orthogonal complement\" and lines 21-22 \"`v_T` orthogonal to both `n\u208a` and `n\u208b`\"), but `B.orthogonal` is Mathlib's right-hand complement: membership means `B n\u208a v = 0 \u2227 B n\u208b v = 0`, not `B v n\u208a = 0`. The module docstring advertises that \"no symmetry of `B` is needed\", so for the intended generality the two sides genuinely differ, and the file itself needs `hB : B.IsRefl` to get the other order.",
   "line": "136"
  }
 ],
